<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=crucible-steel">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crucible steel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Crucible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> is steel made by melting iron, often with fluxes, in a crucible, which allows precise control of its carbon content; it was first developed in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> by the wootz process, with material as early as 300 BCE. Wootz steel was traded widely and became famous in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> steel. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Benjamin Huntsman of Sheffield developed it in parallel from 1740, making the first fully homogeneous steel; within a century Sheffield's output rose from about 200 to over 80,000 tonnes a year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crucible-steel.jpg" alt="Crucible steel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ashley Dace · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crucible_Steel_near_to_Beauchief,_Sheffield,_Great_Britain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 300 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Southern India and Sri Lanka</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Sheffield, England (Benjamin Huntsman) (1740)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Securely proven production sites are later: Sri Lanka in the 6th to 10th centuries CE and southern India from the 16th; the early evidence from Kodumanal is preliminary.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">Iron smelting<span>c. 2500 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=crucible-steel">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
